| $9$ | $3$ | $1$ | $⋯$ | $27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bff7206-3e83-5486-87f5-627306292967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414646} {:answer/id #uuid "385da54c-4492-5bbc-951a-ed5c5080d241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414647} {:answer/id #uuid "c9aa9be2-fd88-5217-83f7-344e4baf419e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414656} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a61390-9440-51e4-aed8-0f77831ed9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414657} {:answer/id #uuid "faeabdd2-c5d4-509e-96e7-7dc4d046eb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "385da54c-4492-5bbc-951a-ed5c5080d241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414647} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e595341-6d0b-5218-827a-e206e85ab469" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44f2ea0b-1adf-505c-8fa7-ec801c70c20d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40560" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{2})(1)+(1)(\\sqrt{2})+(-2)(-5)+(3)(2)}{\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{32}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{32}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bec6782-5b25-5f66-a69c-bd940a684008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414645} {:answer/id #uuid "42d9c80c-b7ce-5329-bd52-05c6944cc99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414648} {:answer/id #uuid "6fccab70-38d0-5196-9e05-328ab9515f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414649} {:answer/id #uuid "82db5a26-f843-545e-b28d-8edda8760ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{87.27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414651} {:answer/id #uuid "89bd71bc-8f74-5cc5-8323-858f0ad1097e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fccab70-38d0-5196-9e05-328ab9515f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414649} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9256cbf-1d22-506c-aaed-b510f33eb670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99b52f99-8be2-5e57-bfd3-38bdea87dcf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-40563" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)(1)+(\\sqrt{5})(-2)+(1)(2\\sqrt{5})+(-1)(3)}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{43}\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{1462}}{1462}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3\\sqrt{1462}}{1462})≈{85.50}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fa685c-26d9-50c3-9545-5630e0f95fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414724} {:answer/id #uuid "194427ad-263d-5d8c-83c2-9e6a530a1d0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414725} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1253b4-be89-5b75-90d7-9bf16f52d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} {:answer/id #uuid "a926e36b-6239-5512-942a-761db7b00ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414740} {:answer/id #uuid "f308a53e-6685-5d66-b2f6-d1b95a0af889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c1253b4-be89-5b75-90d7-9bf16f52d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce877fc-f404-5538-a461-7601462b178c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44c51145-6075-534b-b54c-46371542f3eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40737" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048ef5c8-0a10-53a5-bbcf-1d721a524144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414721} {:answer/id #uuid "05da9795-05f5-5040-9da5-00cee056fe08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414722} {:answer/id #uuid "1326ecd9-681d-55f9-841c-c7013851f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414723} {:answer/id #uuid "292aa1fd-4f1b-5b3f-b502-e340aa086aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414726} {:answer/id #uuid "502fbad5-6108-585e-9a6e-c3c7e917352d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1326ecd9-681d-55f9-841c-c7013851f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414723} :answer-field/id #uuid "219cb7dd-0360-5f51-af53-0c31b7c77c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7bb7823-c38b-5250-a2f7-3b306162dae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40736" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{17}{6},$ $b=\\frac{11}{6},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{17}{6})}{(\\frac{11}{6})}=\\frac{17}{11}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} {:answer/id #uuid "62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415079} {:answer/id #uuid "aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415084} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415085} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9c0c78-b891-5f32-8dca-852634763b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-104732" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t,\\frac{\\pi t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y)\\,i-({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415071} {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} {:answer/id #uuid "75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415080} {:answer/id #uuid "87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415081} {:answer/id #uuid "db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/math-academy-id "q-104637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa14c10-e738-56f6-ba1c-e071a121f17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415109} {:answer/id #uuid "3394fe0c-1ef5-5d2a-acd6-6a75d0644ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415118} {:answer/id #uuid "796589ac-95c1-56f9-b1db-31d0e03ed1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415125} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae1013-f366-5e65-90ff-b5b5ec91675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415128} {:answer/id #uuid "f6952c2b-bc0a-5679-9e51-c3da4a58655d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae1013-f366-5e65-90ff-b5b5ec91675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415128} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed476833-2f74-5fd6-89de-22e15124bc5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d4a87d-4b65-5a4c-9736-928bedaa6703" :question/math-academy-id "q-137086" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $12a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 30\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n12a+\\frac{b}{c}=12\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})+\\frac{3}{1}=-17.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd7515d-bc8c-5fbf-b8b7-be4ee29a026e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415110} {:answer/id #uuid "229bfab5-ba1b-52fe-b0f1-2051f81b3eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415111} {:answer/id #uuid "383d6df1-6a22-5c04-94da-26eb4b0bd68d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415120} {:answer/id #uuid "59b52a2f-1933-53c4-9f7d-6b819b9dbb83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415122} {:answer/id #uuid "7818ba2a-afff-5ecd-8ff6-f5c7aa948efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7818ba2a-afff-5ecd-8ff6-f5c7aa948efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415124} :answer-field/id #uuid "54a4eb9e-e422-55cf-a2cc-fe7a41adc569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4a26f8e-8621-57f0-b0fb-289f901931d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-136954" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{3}{1}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59393bce-26a0-53f4-b9ee-a4b60e0309ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453336} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ec25f8-9101-5ec4-ae2b-1947b4fed11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ec25f8-9101-5ec4-ae2b-1947b4fed11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453337} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aafa3ab-4c0c-5ec8-8508-509f0aa15d9e" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59eb0512-2892-504c-817d-6e1c62f596b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453338} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2be482-8af1-5d7d-8d56-389be603fc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2be482-8af1-5d7d-8d56-389be603fc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453339} :answer-field/id #uuid "359d2acf-2ea6-56b6-a467-a84c75cf4d9e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7762d99b-6748-51f0-9769-d7e020b910e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453340} {:answer/id #uuid "761205e9-2c61-502f-8e9d-e4e3f37407aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7762d99b-6748-51f0-9769-d7e020b910e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453340} :answer-field/id #uuid "95713f76-4916-53c2-b271-58871420022e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0027cfb5-d932-549f-8cc6-973642895e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =e\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a6ae28-ab05-53de-9b3f-5e347ef15023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415194} {:answer/id #uuid "14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} {:answer/id #uuid "32debd00-39de-5d6e-9e96-d38fb37c9e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415208} {:answer/id #uuid "485effef-da1a-51e4-aa09-f8d8650ea3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415214} {:answer/id #uuid "d067cbee-9b2c-5c36-8dc8-c47522a4af35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ad03362-7885-5567-9b35-4c8299722802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c651ce1-2ea6-5480-b7a8-389903a354b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125750" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3},2)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-(1)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066318d7-b3c3-582b-8704-baaa946c3602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415512} {:answer/id #uuid "1a49bca9-5ff7-58a4-97d5-3b205c07eee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415514} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddf6f00-c938-50e9-9408-30046a58b1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415516} {:answer/id #uuid "6426d8d3-9d04-5e0f-a517-240093a9074a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415520} {:answer/id #uuid "8a136f45-dabb-5965-b74e-f1e60e2efbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ddf6f00-c938-50e9-9408-30046a58b1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415516} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b1c789-64a3-55f5-9e7d-ac42dc183f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b858f50b-1a14-576c-973e-c7199e8e8cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161090" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\pi \\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}),$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}).$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415877} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ee986dc-58c5-5a6b-9a45-c8daf6a2b863" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "384a66a5-a5c9-58f8-aa5d-723b50b59cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325130" :question/problem "The fasting glucose level of a clinic’s patients is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and a standard deviation of $15\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Additionally, $60%$ of the patients have a glucose level between $0.92\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and $1.08\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Find the percentage of patients with a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ | $\\,0.0314\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ | $\\,0.0392\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the fasting glucose level of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<130).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.08\\mu  & =12.624 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{12.624}{0.08} \\\\  & ≈157.80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<130)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<130) & =P(Z<\\frac{130-157.80}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.85) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.85) \\\\  & =0.0322 \\\\  & ≈3.22%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.22%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415873} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef719c3-66a7-50b4-a2f7-e921efeb034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415874} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4b0043-0c74-53a6-a993-6328a52b0af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415875} {:answer/id #uuid "58d37435-1075-579f-a1f9-ce0f3925f1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415876} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fee109-4588-538f-b649-9c3fd53fe5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415873} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b712d39-ff45-54f8-b106-03be0d0cda60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "979b0f60-49c9-5b48-86f8-6e501f45d940" :question/math-academy-id "q-95151" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the videos produced by a popular content creator is $35\\,\\text{min}.$ Also, $5%$ of their videos have a duration of less than $20$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of the videos is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the duration times, rounded to the near minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a video. Then we have $X∼N(35,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 20)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-35}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{15}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1097 \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈9\\,\\text{minutes}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f6af28-58a5-5980-8be5-d39c9b4b3514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415894} {:answer/id #uuid "3f58fbfa-2a3c-5f8a-8c15-ac63c162c8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415898} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2e9088-2863-5032-aa2c-88ec65760072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415908} {:answer/id #uuid "b6449307-a0ba-56cc-a5f1-291146f541f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415913} {:answer/id #uuid "e8895199-6569-5bad-a980-9e6b74e4814d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f58fbfa-2a3c-5f8a-8c15-ac63c162c8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415898} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4962c93-3582-5793-94a8-d5e64919a899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0071fbbf-507b-5e86-9e07-511fe6080666" :question/math-academy-id "q-161183" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\sqrt{1+4x}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1+4x}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a459a9807befc312528771626dca293a7ebbccd97a2c1e938f47a2d90a36726.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3})=2\\sqrt{x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(2\\sqrt{x})}^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1+4x}}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{1+4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x+2{x}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2+8)-0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1c8e0a-12da-57b1-b178-119e2ed0094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415889} {:answer/id #uuid "134a8ec5-3c99-5a35-b135-9550a1a589e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415890} {:answer/id #uuid "4281a994-2dee-5d00-a661-565bf6f7aacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi (\\sqrt{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415899} {:answer/id #uuid "543c20d4-2012-58fc-988d-4183a7a0d1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi (2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415902} {:answer/id #uuid "f67ff004-d369-5080-8ecc-4238d9b7be10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543c20d4-2012-58fc-988d-4183a7a0d1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi (2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415902} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42dd57a-1d8c-5adc-b40d-c45195678c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "139ae790-9731-55fc-ab5e-266ae6726353" :question/math-academy-id "q-109765" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z=xy$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=xy,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0faa92e0f89c86248abffad7f61cac5d94a3848a7f7be4464c4b28d3915aa32.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y)^{2}+(x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}\\,{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}[{2}^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi (2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1735e166-5acc-5324-851d-91faeb7a9618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415891} {:answer/id #uuid "3369dcb6-e91a-5e4b-b5d1-596236417674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16(x+y)}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,$ and $\\,y\\ge 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415895} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba101ca-c17d-55ee-8ca8-392a0b3b4fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{16}{{y}^{2}}}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}<4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415900} {:answer/id #uuid "d803556f-449c-5597-ae86-ae738f26a508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,$ and $\\,y\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415920} {:answer/id #uuid "f8918b25-32d9-5a19-ba97-973014cfd089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{17}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8918b25-32d9-5a19-ba97-973014cfd089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{17}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415926} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b2b5a8a-3d1e-588b-a0ea-de39882b51aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d2fc887-f2a2-5a12-af93-46ac7487f8f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161442" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the cone $y=4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ that lies inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}})=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}})=\\frac{4z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}+\\frac{16{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{17({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{17}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dc37952-1697-5509-8bc7-6ccd85ec6880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415897} {:answer/id #uuid "aea19d27-cea1-5633-a35f-860030bc1eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (\\sqrt{14}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415911} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f9a248-c377-505f-ac3a-9e96059ce3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi (\\sqrt{17}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415912} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b6f195-b9dc-56ca-a43b-6af6cf36c33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415917} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a1588-1e59-59e9-802e-6ebad0b0fea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a1588-1e59-59e9-802e-6ebad0b0fea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415918} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2bc578f-8afe-5a87-8c83-39f18d34c660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a9f44f1-362f-50d2-bbcf-0383dfbcd3ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-161175" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $y=3x+2z$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 16,\\,x\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=3x+2z,\\,(x,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 16,\\,x\\le 0,\\,z\\ge 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48ffe2b0bd40937205d9b9d3b6cc264f83bd56ea30a5df318ba7c7507efb68f7.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+2z)=3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3x+2z)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{14}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa6f07d-e7c8-523d-bd08-943563edb16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415892} {:answer/id #uuid "7853d8da-02ca-52fe-82ab-8d3e95436d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+18{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415904} {:answer/id #uuid "944324b3-9b00-578a-a869-20197fc9cd6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2+36{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415906} {:answer/id #uuid "a7727358-0e8f-5186-a4f4-a4093c7f5305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+6{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415910} {:answer/id #uuid "d52761a0-f3c2-5486-8780-7ee4cfd846a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "944324b3-9b00-578a-a869-20197fc9cd6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2+36{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415906} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ec2bea1-f597-57e9-8493-155da2b72ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "795a5978-6945-51dd-9230-522e60fb9dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161129" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z=3{x}^{2}-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3{x}^{2}-y,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-y)=6x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(6x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+36{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{2+36{x}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4ce4b5-b090-52fb-8521-91a7d5fb6e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415896} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5b7aca-0852-5446-be6b-4b27132d1d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415903} {:answer/id #uuid "89d97de7-0a07-5bd0-a64a-ce2c1b74eb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415905} {:answer/id #uuid "bb48da7b-7426-5584-96a0-a5b3e5a67853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415915} {:answer/id #uuid "c3100f75-563f-5f1f-9d25-aade507ac5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a4ce4b5-b090-52fb-8521-91a7d5fb6e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415896} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c69fee-4df2-5b99-a8fc-ad508d7794be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3090cbd-55f4-50b9-b96a-e12ca57ae315" :question/math-academy-id "q-161226" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x=\\frac{y-z}{3}$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by the constraints $1\\le y\\le 4$ and $1\\le z\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y-z}{3},\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,1\\le z\\le 3},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f06a9c686a935cb6c561affbcd224045618d9cacaf94eb7c9eab8a9ca8da44c.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y-z}{3})=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{y-z}{3})=-\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{9}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03199862-16b9-557b-8bef-764c1954ae8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415888} {:answer/id #uuid "96e2a9a0-2d62-5a79-a93d-b7db07b18154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415907} {:answer/id #uuid "b9882397-20a7-55a5-b9af-85aae783bc47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415914} {:answer/id #uuid "f20ee68d-f941-5858-910c-aad522ef84b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415924} {:answer/id #uuid "fcddb203-3aef-5988-8495-b140e1e4214e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f20ee68d-f941-5858-910c-aad522ef84b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415924} :answer-field/id #uuid "989c6007-badd-544d-87f7-194be590ca1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0057193-1fc8-5f60-b2e8-92515ea3b1e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161153" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2\\sqrt{5}x+2y$ that is bounded by the constraints $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{5}x+2y,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{5}x+2y)=2\\sqrt{5}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{5}x+2y)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(2\\sqrt{5})}^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+20+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246b6bfe-20aa-55cc-b239-3304de81494f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415952} {:answer/id #uuid "72fa2757-f8e2-52c7-97eb-f3ea64fd0de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415959} {:answer/id #uuid "a9391385-1fbe-53da-993e-c0f38007e6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415964} {:answer/id #uuid "abd564cc-a7f0-5f60-a224-edcb8733b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415965} {:answer/id #uuid "fa49e0e8-2a63-57cc-8df5-3f02f87ef7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd564cc-a7f0-5f60-a224-edcb8733b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415965} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a33e921-a9bc-5edf-9d0c-b149f91622e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e3b0ae8-2464-5f3a-ba5b-28ac6b071959" :question/math-academy-id "q-161326" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}y,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}y\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2\\sqrt{2}y\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137ced3c-e27e-55d0-a129-d7f6d14ddfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415947} {:answer/id #uuid "1dee489f-a3c9-5e67-9010-8a2bb6344bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415950} {:answer/id #uuid "24d68779-dfc9-5e3b-9438-879f14f3462b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415953} {:answer/id #uuid "6354a1eb-c050-5050-93ac-b70e93b7b31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415956} {:answer/id #uuid "ba006680-3290-5c38-89b3-8d6c40bc62cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "137ced3c-e27e-55d0-a129-d7f6d14ddfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415947} :answer-field/id #uuid "867991c0-ff4c-568a-b34d-41463d47a5c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44573c5a-fe32-5ee3-9d3f-c65bffe1a9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161342" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x(0-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20eaebd4-47b4-5a06-b816-cc531888309e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415951} {:answer/id #uuid "3829b03c-0d7f-5b85-969e-c475546e2d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{395}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415955} {:answer/id #uuid "6a510a5b-13c1-5f24-b8c2-ff019d89d2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415957} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2d7ec7-e4c0-578d-84f7-8e6cf8e46b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415966} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c0bc18-5385-5a20-91df-0c00a7144747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{79}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2c0bc18-5385-5a20-91df-0c00a7144747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{79}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415967} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d471815-2895-5fb8-bed5-64d74c7a07a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6d27cd-0aea-5d32-8f1f-4a62535f9e3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161351" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(24xy+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=3x-4y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-4y)=3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(24xy+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(24xy+(3x-4y)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{∬}_{D}\\,(24xy+9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-24xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{∬}_{D}\\,(9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[9{x}^{2}y+\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(9{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{[3{x}^{3}+\\frac{16}{3}x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}(24+\\frac{32}{3}-3-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{3}\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008daf1a-a433-5118-aad3-61bc2cd82151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415943} {:answer/id #uuid "11622f2c-a064-5989-bfd9-037259b1990a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415945} {:answer/id #uuid "31b4ae36-f11f-5826-afc4-9e1f22cf793d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415954} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac59834-bde8-5e44-938a-c936359f2042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415961} {:answer/id #uuid "a786601a-73a7-5725-8042-296bce616964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11622f2c-a064-5989-bfd9-037259b1990a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415945} :answer-field/id #uuid "105048ab-2d03-5b31-abc9-b516f5aaa13b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c04d8b1-030c-5d18-9c3c-13b32378d108" :question/math-academy-id "q-161316" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b1b263baf01b692a0344388207c12d3c4dac0f49d11cd4bfa744f2841df2c6b.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the fourth quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =9[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1-1) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193323db-4837-5978-b14e-f2ac7216eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415948} {:answer/id #uuid "6a54bfb9-103b-535a-a0de-5777c5154e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415958} {:answer/id #uuid "a40290ed-721f-5496-959a-a2ae7e6cf644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415962} {:answer/id #uuid "c8beac62-5cb9-5816-b59e-39200a1a65b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415970} {:answer/id #uuid "cb82683b-e2da-594a-840f-2f2e47c75562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a40290ed-721f-5496-959a-a2ae7e6cf644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415962} :answer-field/id #uuid "33f085d7-1a2e-5ed1-949e-eab11615591e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac50dc50-55cb-5157-8205-32f49dbad931" :question/math-academy-id "q-161338" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe04241f55bae9dae231be6486a3c53985c8dc4af9e9656787267d9d852f829e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{3}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}(1-{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44790b19-9ced-5d46-bcb9-825594809176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416157} {:answer/id #uuid "6215fee1-a677-5186-a3d4-62cb7223b14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416171} {:answer/id #uuid "b234e6b8-43be-5423-be11-ece81f246d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416204} {:answer/id #uuid "b60dbd39-c84e-5d27-aee9-c2f06ea806f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416207} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9a351-f8a5-58dc-901e-b298299b5029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b234e6b8-43be-5423-be11-ece81f246d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416204} :answer-field/id #uuid "915e3c86-f8a0-5f78-befd-32698839d7d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdc532fe-5376-523b-b4c1-4852cb3ef3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-163704" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-\\frac{1}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-\\frac{1}{0+1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=1-\\frac{1}{t+1}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=3\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(3)=1-\\frac{1}{3+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c81834-42ef-5852-b301-a6d3cbb3c8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416327} {:answer/id #uuid "970d7f5e-257f-5c6c-a87e-441a641de9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416338} {:answer/id #uuid "9f06a3b6-8e48-5d5d-b47c-2bf33ee89c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416339} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4520b1-1d35-57bb-b9ad-3e48af9ee8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416349} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6421c2-2bc0-520a-be99-675b8f795b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10c81834-42ef-5852-b301-a6d3cbb3c8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416327} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54a21cb-93a2-537e-bb2f-71cc7631af9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f199eb7-3af6-57bb-a681-cef6be591a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139476" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{8}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fef03fc-79ed-52dc-bd31-1e9cf734dd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416329} {:answer/id #uuid "5170028a-ab43-580a-a8b5-29da72f1fcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416333} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a4aea-dd6d-5e0a-99e0-02a535cf0fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416336} {:answer/id #uuid "c52aa776-edc2-57f3-a183-a9201feae1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416345} {:answer/id #uuid "f37da0e2-81e8-576a-aa5e-b3594802c8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5170028a-ab43-580a-a8b5-29da72f1fcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416333} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5f218f-38ba-531a-af9e-df5b58a66d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6dcfad9-4fa8-567a-ab9a-f61e9f6b1a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-39856" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416527} {:answer/id #uuid "50b04112-6a4b-5de0-8b36-cac7c5a0a40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416544} {:answer/id #uuid "5a88c79a-d8b2-5e43-8a47-5013ebd4e46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416548} {:answer/id #uuid "945a35b8-1727-56f0-8d1f-a7a7ff35591a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416564} {:answer/id #uuid "a2edcdc2-4bd9-58b5-8e0b-973a0e0009a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416527} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ec3541-b791-5354-9296-f775e0ff2635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f239f11-8e8b-53a2-a96f-22cc233efad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-113526" :question/problem "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b26590d-792e-51f9-b6ee-9b92d34732ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416538} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ac9c4-89a8-53e0-9f5b-0281ad0e261f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416539} {:answer/id #uuid "419a7dcc-0ec9-5201-aaac-a0d26fd4a862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416542} {:answer/id #uuid "57bd0aaf-7e37-5d1f-aa28-5056aba60ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416546} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416574} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb6c09b-dbdd-53f8-b483-27b433b6c2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9fbd56e-ebad-5e95-b783-252de92d505c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36358" :question/problem "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $S$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020bc723-6af1-5316-aaf4-e9de001b448a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7422" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416618} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1b048e-b282-560b-8713-335a9004952e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416619} {:answer/id #uuid "693ff683-c441-5136-b7ec-abf843bb1296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2877" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416627} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd73452-7201-5d58-8fb9-e272ec4a7b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7329" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416634} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416636} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef41a7-faaf-5672-935c-ff24abe14152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c95c5475-5100-54a8-b160-4567ec570e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143339" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(0,{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 15,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\ge 1),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15} & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}) \\\\  & =N(0,\\frac{12}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge 1).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-0}{\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.65) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.65) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.65)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.65)=0.7422.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=1-Φ(0.65)=0.2578.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab934dba-e3a2-5d59-ae83-e95f06179b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472700} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7b43a7-81e3-5385-a0ab-8aadc7cfbe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f7b43a7-81e3-5385-a0ab-8aadc7cfbe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472701} :answer-field/id #uuid "d026cafa-0a30-51b7-99c1-cb46a04536a8" :answer-field/key "$max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063a719d-4200-54e4-9e1b-9c0087bfc4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472702} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ed26bd-c371-53d5-9888-89c529c6ff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ed26bd-c371-53d5-9888-89c529c6ff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472703} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d049014-59a4-5655-9e05-070f06635b97" :answer-field/key "$\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864619e5-82e3-5fc8-9c87-5b622c8d6d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472704} {:answer/id #uuid "b70d53e4-20bc-5813-bb56-65a2b340805a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864619e5-82e3-5fc8-9c87-5b622c8d6d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472704} :answer-field/id #uuid "736ceb2f-5c2e-5a72-8e8c-82929b1731e1" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu })}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ccfb8c1-3a4b-5435-8003-dc3e546488df" :question/math-academy-id "q-139813" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ and $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu })}^{2}$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$; $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13212333-e1f8-56ff-a8c7-3198d8cdf406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417028} {:answer/id #uuid "52814006-a528-5e8d-b83f-56a2cb50f1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417034} {:answer/id #uuid "85946e32-9875-5ae9-b212-a1791298f603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417040} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a0bf2c-4e79-5ebe-80ea-3bd71ad1bd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417042} {:answer/id #uuid "b252df5d-3385-541f-8009-fb9c2955cb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85946e32-9875-5ae9-b212-a1791298f603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417040} :answer-field/id #uuid "1acb675f-5ff9-554d-a90a-36c18f959baf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86915a7f-cf68-50dc-aafb-5f62c6d2e7e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163020" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad5a7d1121f6ae5a471f78477a8857b75c6a5a2008e769b1b9115c2ac880d5b6.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{9}{2}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}},\\,a=1,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{8}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{8}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}]}_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{93}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{93}{5})}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{62}{15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3665ab07-2fe3-53da-8bd4-219390934492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417131} {:answer/id #uuid "88490a55-b515-5dba-8dd0-a984dd302a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417150} {:answer/id #uuid "9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417155} {:answer/id #uuid "ad75aa8f-a4c6-595d-8e09-373c61a5ec3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417156} {:answer/id #uuid "c69c82e9-2236-526a-ae72-3c203db881f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417155} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4b0bd2-0e38-5371-ace1-939725dda131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e28dac56-d247-5873-8ab6-f1ab402c91a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113612" :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5782b94b-8258-5e43-85df-a8d0c1327515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{455}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418073} {:answer/id #uuid "585f608f-5a28-539a-9ae1-19a60685c3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{155}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418074} {:answer/id #uuid "d098068f-0f1e-5a9a-b34b-77d4043a3eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{315}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418077} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6e32fe-4a49-5537-a523-6b3d99454374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{415}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418078} {:answer/id #uuid "f963b964-0ba8-5f1c-b778-714106f60c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{255}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5782b94b-8258-5e43-85df-a8d0c1327515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{455}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418073} :answer-field/id #uuid "9520dcff-aa35-56e0-9ddc-bd7600942c23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "098ae999-41d1-5d21-93d9-672720b112c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-84199" :question/problem "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair  six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $\\text{Var}[X]?$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We can compute the variance of a discrete random variable $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{8})+2(\\frac{3}{8})+3(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{8})+5(\\frac{1}{16})+6(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4)+{5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{8})+4(\\frac{3}{8})+9(\\frac{1}{4})+16(\\frac{1}{8})+25(\\frac{1}{16})+36(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{155}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{155}{16}-{(\\frac{45}{16})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{155}{16}-\\frac{2025}{256} \\\\  & =\\frac{455}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cb8fc2-022b-568c-b41a-7bbf10255e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418072} {:answer/id #uuid "637b3062-8acc-5b54-9040-15b955fa09f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418075} {:answer/id #uuid "96670029-c0b7-54da-8a19-99c6872c02f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418076} {:answer/id #uuid "f3700714-cf33-5fb7-9887-c4e1c1ca2d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418079} {:answer/id #uuid "fef72be8-42ee-5d66-9f10-03ce259c9db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3700714-cf33-5fb7-9887-c4e1c1ca2d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418079} :answer-field/id #uuid "735318e8-b065-526f-8046-9b9ea9da16fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b04f202f-7340-5122-b2d5-59ca0d357302" :question/math-academy-id "q-129104" :question/problem "Two fair  four-sided dice are rolled and $X$ is the absolute difference of their scores. The probability mass function $f(x)$ of $X$ is shown in the table below. What is $\\text{Var}[X]?$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{2}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |" :question/worked-solution "The variance of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{2}{8})+1(\\frac{3}{8})+2(\\frac{2}{8})+3(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the variance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{(x-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & ={(0-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{(1-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{(2-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{(3-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{(\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{25}{16})(\\frac{2}{8})+(\\frac{1}{16})(\\frac{3}{8})+(\\frac{9}{16})(\\frac{2}{8})+(\\frac{49}{16})(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4717ce-46ec-5dfd-a87b-f3dbc1bd41c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418284} {:answer/id #uuid "4e35a48b-83ad-5a61-a3f1-7f195f1064b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418289} {:answer/id #uuid "53fe27ad-e859-55d8-9328-3819e6132d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418291} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8135e4-0878-5169-b0ef-52cf2b36e46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418301} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e3297-c505-59cf-87e9-2b40589a0852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e35a48b-83ad-5a61-a3f1-7f195f1064b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418289} :answer-field/id #uuid "fad179a2-756e-591e-8afb-194232c3b6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12a69b61-bda4-5cdc-b9c6-93090eb9484f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94833" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(2,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<-1)=0.3783,$ find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3821\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-1)=0.3783\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le -1)=0.3783\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =0.3783 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-1-2}{\\sigma }) & =0.3783 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) & =0.3783 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.3783)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.31)=0.3783\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.3783)=-0.31.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =-0.31 \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈9.68\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c5beaa-2bd5-5a17-8ed7-0f68efb5d37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418285} {:answer/id #uuid "37635532-f28a-53e6-ae9d-3b79e5b093b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418287} {:answer/id #uuid "6d43d3be-0f5b-5f12-af73-e68463fd21f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418295} {:answer/id #uuid "d88c6cd0-f17d-5079-9478-4d437c156ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418300} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c68472-5317-5646-8c48-47295237b963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37635532-f28a-53e6-ae9d-3b79e5b093b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418287} :answer-field/id #uuid "e61ff356-8ad3-5164-a0c6-b21e9a847a8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "237d3289-35f7-5376-a92f-b552192446e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94890" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-0.8\\mu <X<2.8\\mu )=0.7458,$ find $\\mu ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-0.8\\mu <X<2.8\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -1.8\\mu <X<\\mu +1.8\\mu )=0.7458,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-0.8\\mu <X<2.8\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -1.8\\mu <X<\\mu +1.8\\mu ) & =0.7458 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.8\\mu ) & =0.7458 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<2.8\\mu ) & =0.3729 \\\\ P(X<2.8\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3729 \\\\ P(X<2.8\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3729 \\\\ P(X<2.8\\mu ) & =0.8729 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.8\\mu ) & =0.8729\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.8\\mu ) & =0.8729 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.8\\mu -\\mu }{5}) & =0.8729 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.8\\mu }{5}) & =0.8729 \\\\ \\frac{1.8\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8729)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.14)=0.8729\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8729)=1.14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.8\\mu }{5} & =1.14 \\\\ 1.8\\mu  & =5.7 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈3.166\\,67 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈3.17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cdaf6c6-dec3-5b38-a91c-2be375299096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cdaf6c6-dec3-5b38-a91c-2be375299096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418297} :answer-field/id #uuid "0824e9dc-c8d0-5712-a1a3-cb7ecbf153de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4375ac91-f4dd-57cf-abab-b28e66baa865" :question/math-academy-id "q-342034" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<3)=0.5468,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<3)=P(-3<X<3).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<X<3) & =0.5468 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3) & =0.5468 \\\\ P(0<X<3) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<3)-P(X<0) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<3)-0.5 & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<3) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(X\\le 3) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =0.75 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{0.75} \\\\ \\sigma  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(4)^{2}=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
